-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookups are served from a local embedded SQLite index that the kiosk builds from each snapshot it imports. The index is private to this node: its file location, schema, and queries are not visible to any other node.
+
+## Index
+
+Each imported snapshot becomes one SQLite database file on kiosk storage. An index file is built once, at import, and is never modified afterwards.
+
+- **Records table**: one row per snapshot record, keyed by normalized serial number (see Lookup Behavior), carrying the equipment description, calibration date, and due date.
+- **Snapshot metadata**: the snapshot date and schema version of the snapshot the file was built from.
+
+Because the snapshot date lives in the same file as the records, a lookup result's record and snapshot date always come from the same snapshot.
+
+Exactly one index file is active at a time. The active file is the one at a fixed path inside the registry's storage directory; a file being built sits at a separate staging path in the same directory, so that activation is a rename within one filesystem.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Import runs in this order, and stops at the first failure:
+
+1. Verify the signature manifest against the snapshot files using the bundled public key. No record is parsed before the signature verifies.
+2. Reject a schema version the kiosk does not support.
+3. Build a new index file at the staging path, reading the snapshot records one at a time rather than loading the snapshot into memory. A record whose normalized serial number is already present in the staging index fails the import as a duplicate.
+4. Run SQLite's integrity check on the completed staging file.
+5. Activate: rename the staging file over the active path.
+
+The active index is untouched until step 5, so lookups keep answering from the previous snapshot throughout an import. A lookup already in progress when activation happens completes against the snapshot it started on; every lookup that starts after activation sees the new snapshot in full.
+
+An import failure at any step deletes the staging file, leaves the previous snapshot active, and reports which step failed and why (invalid signature, unsupported schema version with the version found, duplicate serial with the serial, unreadable media, insufficient storage). At startup, a staging file left behind by an interrupted import is deleted before any lookup is served.
+
+Only one import runs at a time.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. The normalized serial is looked up as a single primary-key query on a read-only connection to the active index file. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+A single keyed read over 90,000 rows is well inside the 250 ms lookup goal on the 4 GB kiosk; the index needs no warm-up and holds no in-memory copy of the registry.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Both alternatives answer lookups over a network, which the kiosks do not have during operation and which the project tenets rule out for lookup. SQLite gives predictable keyed lookups from one local file with no service to run. The cost is that every kiosk validates and builds its own index when a snapshot arrives. |
+| Where the index is built | On the kiosk, from the signed snapshot records | Ship a prebuilt SQLite file inside the snapshot | The signature covers the records the inventory team published; building locally means the kiosk only ever trusts what it verified, and the snapshot format stays independent of the index schema. |
+| Replacing the active snapshot | Build a separate file, then rename it over the active path | Replace rows inside the active database in one transaction | A rename is all-or-nothing and leaves the previous file byte-for-byte intact until it succeeds, which is what a failed import must leave behind. An in-place transaction puts the only good copy at risk for the whole length of the import. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether the kiosk keeps the previous index file after activation, to allow going back to it.
+2. Whether an import of a snapshot older than the active one is accepted.
